{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.StokesSingularCountAffine","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.StokesSingularCountAffine","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.StokesSingularCountAffine.stokes_canonical_not_surfaceTerm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.StokesSingularCountAffine.stokes_canonical_not_surfaceTerm","kind":"theorem","line":178,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/StokesSingularCountAffine.lean#L178-L189","doc":"**Under (H1), the Stokes singular count of the canonical closure is not a surface term.** The\nhypothesis `hsing` IS (H1): a singular count that respects Euler's bound `≥ 9A + 1` eventually.\nOver the Seifert cell count `15A − 1` its ratio is bounded below by something tending to `3/5`,\nso it fails `StokesSurfaceTerm`. Like the hyperbolic ball, the Stokes route then renormalises the\nvolume coefficient and supplies no `A^(−1/3)`. Nothing here discharges `hsing`; see the header. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.StokesSingularCountAffine.seifertCells","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.StokesSingularCountAffine.seifertCells","kind":"def","line":175,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/StokesSingularCountAffine.lean#L175-L176","doc":"The Seifert surface's cell count for the canonical closure: `3A` disks and `12A − 1` bands. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.StokesSingularCountAffine.not_stokesSurfaceTerm_of_bounds","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.StokesSingularCountAffine.not_stokesSurfaceTerm_of_bounds","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/StokesSingularCountAffine.lean#L131-L171","doc":"**The general criterion.** If the singular count is eventually at least an affine function with\npositive slope, and the cell count is eventually positive and at most an affine function, then the\nratio does not tend to `0`, so the pair is not a `StokesSurfaceTerm`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.StokesSingularCountAffine.chi_of_genus_boundary","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.StokesSingularCountAffine.chi_of_genus_boundary","kind":"theorem","line":122,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/StokesSingularCountAffine.lean#L122-L127","doc":"Euler's formula in the form `χ = 2 − 2g − μ` for a compact orientable surface of genus `g` with\n`μ` boundary components, together with the corpus's `2g = 8A`, `μ = A + 1`, reproduces\n`χ = −9A + 1` — the three measured invariants are mutually consistent. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.StokesSingularCountAffine.edges_ge_of_chi_canonical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.StokesSingularCountAffine.edges_ge_of_chi_canonical","kind":"theorem","line":115,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/StokesSingularCountAffine.lean#L115-L120","doc":"**At the canonical closure's `χ = −9A + 1`, every decomposition has `≥ 9A + 1` edges.** The\n1-skeleton of any cell decomposition grows at least linearly in `A`. (Whether the connection's\nsingular locus contains it is hypothesis (H1) of the header, not a consequence.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.StokesSingularCountAffine.edges_ge_two_sub_chi","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.StokesSingularCountAffine.edges_ge_two_sub_chi","kind":"theorem","line":110,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/StokesSingularCountAffine.lean#L110-L113","doc":"**Any cell decomposition with a vertex and a face has at least `2 − χ` edges.** Pure Euler. ","signatureChunk":0}]}